1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,694
If you've ever freelanced, you know the worst part of an overdue
invoice.

2
00:00:04,644 --> 00:00:06,591
It's the asking.

3
00:00:06,591 --> 00:00:11,118
You have to go back to someone you like and ask for money you already
earned.

4
00:00:11,118 --> 00:00:13,420
Kindly does that part.

5
00:00:13,420 --> 00:00:18,801
It chases the client, negotiates the terms, and takes the payment
through PayPal.

6
00:00:19,410 --> 00:00:22,214
Here's a studio's actual book.

7
00:00:22,164 --> 00:00:26,045
Eleven thousand five hundred fifty dollars still out.

8
00:00:26,045 --> 00:00:28,664
Three invoices past due.

9
00:00:28,664 --> 00:00:32,177
Nine hundred that already came in through PayPal.

10
00:00:32,177 --> 00:00:36,664
Before Kindly does anything, it has to work inside these limits.

11
00:00:36,664 --> 00:00:38,953
Two contacts a week.

12
00:00:38,953 --> 00:00:42,164
Never under seventy percent up front.

13
00:00:42,164 --> 00:00:44,795
No discounts without asking.

14
00:00:44,795 --> 00:00:48,875
Three instalments at most, inside forty-five days.

15
00:00:48,875 --> 00:00:50,953
You write them once.

16
00:00:50,953 --> 00:00:53,638
The agent can't go around them.

17
00:00:54,820 --> 00:01:00,441
Acme's accounts-payable agent can release twelve hundred dollars
without waking anyone up.

18
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:03,745
Kindly asks for sixteen eighty up front.

19
00:01:03,745 --> 00:01:09,364
That counter breaks your seventy percent floor, so the policy engine
turns it down.

20
00:01:09,364 --> 00:01:11,811
No model gets a vote.

21
00:01:11,811 --> 00:01:17,442
Kindly holds, their agent goes to its finance lead, and both sides
wait for a person.

22
00:01:17,442 --> 00:01:21,534
Every message here is signed, and the signatures check out.

23
00:01:22,149 --> 00:01:24,862
Their finance lead says yes.

24
00:01:24,813 --> 00:01:30,641
Kindly splits the remaining seven hundred twenty dollars into two
dated PayPal invoices.

25
00:01:30,641 --> 00:01:33,588
Each one gets its own payment link.

26
00:01:38,289 --> 00:01:41,989
The original invoice is still what the client owes.

27
00:01:41,939 --> 00:01:47,057
The instalments sit underneath it, so the same money never gets
counted twice.

28
00:01:47,057 --> 00:01:51,741
They exist in PayPal as real invoices, with their own payer links.

29
00:01:52,349 --> 00:01:54,813
The client gets one link.

30
00:01:54,763 --> 00:01:57,367
No account, no login.

31
00:01:57,367 --> 00:02:01,381
If they want different terms, they type it in their own words.

32
00:02:01,381 --> 00:02:07,025
Kindly works out what they're asking, checks it against your limits,
and writes back in your voice.

33
00:02:07,629 --> 00:02:11,736
When the payment lands, PayPal sends a signed webhook.

34
00:02:11,687 --> 00:02:15,581
The invoice closes, and the receipt is already there.

35
00:02:16,189 --> 00:02:19,429
Then there's the part I care about most.

36
00:02:19,378 --> 00:02:22,878
Kindly tells you what actually caused the payment.

37
00:02:22,878 --> 00:02:28,813
This one, the client paid on their own, ten days ahead of their usual,
and Kindly says so.

38
00:02:28,813 --> 00:02:31,234
It doesn't take the credit.

39
00:02:31,234 --> 00:02:38,024
The same ledger keeps the refusal, with the rule it broke, so you can
check the agent's work instead of trusting it.

40
00:02:38,650 --> 00:02:42,771
Kindly does the asking, the negotiating and the settling.

41
00:02:42,721 --> 00:02:45,352
The money moves through PayPal.

42
00:02:45,352 --> 00:02:48,523
And it shows you why each invoice got paid.

